\title[Peeling profiles: rigidity, stability, universality]{Peeling Profiles:\\
Rigidity, Stability, and the Universal Family\\ of Extremal Dilation Peelings}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{20 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A \emph{peeling process} is the abstract skeleton shared by a wide family of
geometric and combinatorial arguments: one removes successive layers from an
object and records the residual content. The data reduces to a nonincreasing
nonnegative sequence $s_0 \ge s_1 \ge \cdots \ge 0$ whose successive
differences $g_k = s_k - s_{k+1}$ are the layer contents. The classical upper
bound is a pigeonhole statement: within any window of $N$ steps there is a
step with $g_k \le \rho_N := (s_0-s_N)/N$. We develop the sharpness theory of
this bound. We prove a four-way rigidity equivalence identifying saturation of
the bound with exact equipartition, with affinity of the profile, and with
invariance of the layer contents under the cyclic shift of the window; more
generally invariance under any pretransitive group action forces extremality,
and extremality is equivalent to invariance under the full symmetric group. We
prove a linear, dimension-free stability estimate that degenerates to rigidity.
We give a variational description through an exact energy identity, exhibiting
the extremisers as the minimisers of the sum of squared layer contents.
Finally we construct the matching geometric family in complete generality: for
every dimension $d \ge 1$, every $N \ge 1$ and \emph{every} measurable
star-shaped body $K \subseteq \R^d$ of finite volume, the dilates $c_kK$ with
$c_k = (1-k/N)^{1/d}$ peel $K$ into $N$ layers of equal volume $\vol(K)/N$,
each layer is invariant under the entire linear symmetry group of $K$, and
conversely any dilation peeling with all layers of volume at most $\vol(K)/N$
must be this one. The factors $c_k$ depend on $d$ and $N$ only, never on $K$.
We also show that the outermost equal-volume shell of a ball in $\R^d$ has
thickness at most $R/(d(N-1))$, so high-dimensional equal-volume peelings
collapse onto the boundary sphere.
\end{abstract}

